    term: 'First Term',
    contentHTML:
      '\n<div class="learn-intro">\n  <span class="learn-keyword">Farm surveying</span> is the process of measuring and mapping land to determine its size, shape, boundaries, and features before it is put to agricultural use. It is the first practical step in <span class="learn-keyword">farm planning</span>, which is the deliberate organisation of farm resources (land, labour, capital) to achieve maximum, sustainable output.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">1. Meaning and Importance of Farm Surveying</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">Farm surveying involves taking measurements of land area, boundaries, contours, and existing features (roads, streams, buildings) so that an accurate map or plan of the farm can be drawn. It is important because it helps in accurate land measurement for ownership and taxation, proper layout of farm structures and crop fields, drainage and irrigation planning, and the preparation of farm plans and maps for record-keeping and loan applications.</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">2. Instruments Used in Farm Surveying</h3>\n<div class="learn-table-wrap">\n  <table class="learn-table">\n    <thead><tr><th>Instrument</th><th>Use</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Chain / Tape</td><td>Measuring distances/lengths on the ground</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Ranging pole</td><td>Marking straight lines and sighting points</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Prismatic compass</td><td>Measuring bearings/direction</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Theodolite</td><td>Measuring horizontal and vertical angles precisely</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Surveyor\'s level (dumpy level)</td><td>Determining differences in elevation/contours</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Pegs and arrows</td><td>Marking survey stations and chain lengths</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Field book</td><td>Recording field measurements and sketches</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">3. Methods of Land Measurement</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">Common methods include <strong>chain surveying</strong> (using a chain/tape to measure straight lines between fixed points), <strong>compass surveying</strong> (using bearings taken with a prismatic compass), and <strong>plane table surveying</strong> (plotting the map directly in the field on a table). Modern farms may also use GPS-based surveying for speed and accuracy.</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">4. Uses of Surveying Data in Agriculture</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">Survey data is used to calculate the exact farm size for planning cropping patterns and stocking rates, to design irrigation and drainage channels along the correct gradient, to site farm buildings and roads appropriately, to demarcate boundaries and prevent land disputes, and to prepare farm maps required for land titling and agricultural loans.</p>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Do not confuse a <em>theodolite</em> (measures angles) with a <em>surveyor\'s level</em> (measures elevation differences). WAEC often tests instrument-function matching.\n</div>\n',
