    contentHTML:
      '\n<div class="learn-intro">\n  <span class="learn-keyword">Farm planning</span> is the deliberate arrangement of a farmer\'s resources — land, labour, capital and management — over a period of time to achieve set production objectives efficiently and profitably.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">1. Definition and Objectives of Farm Planning</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">Farm planning is a systematic process of deciding what to produce, how much to produce, and how resources will be organised to achieve maximum output at minimum cost. Its objectives include maximising profit, ensuring efficient use of land, labour and capital, minimising risks and wastage, and achieving food security and sustainability.</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">2. Factors Influencing Farm Planning</h3>\n<div class="learn-table-wrap">\n  <table class="learn-table">\n    <thead><tr><th>Factor</th><th>Explanation</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Size of farm/capital</td><td>Determines scale of operations possible</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Climate and soil</td><td>Determines suitable crops/livestock</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Labour availability</td><td>Affects choice of enterprise and mechanisation level</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Market demand</td><td>Guides what to produce for profit</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Government policy</td><td>Subsidies, land tenure laws, credit facilities</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Farmer\'s objectives</td><td>Subsistence vs commercial production goals</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">3. Steps in Farm Planning</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">The typical steps are: (1) collecting information about farm resources, (2) setting clear objectives, (3) identifying possible enterprises/alternatives, (4) budgeting and estimating costs and returns for each alternative, (5) choosing the best combination of enterprises, and (6) implementing and periodically reviewing the plan.</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">4. Importance of Farm Planning to Agricultural Production</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">Good farm planning ensures efficient allocation of scarce resources, reduces waste and risk, improves productivity and profitability, helps secure credit facilities (as plans are often required by lenders), and provides a basis for monitoring and evaluating farm performance over time.</p>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> Farm planning takes farm surveying data and turns it into a decision-making tool — what to grow, how much, and how resources will be combined — guided by climate, market, labour, capital and the farmer\'s own objectives.\n</div>\n',
