    contentHTML:
      '\n<div class="learn-intro">\n  <span class="learn-keyword">Forest management</span> is the practice of controlling and using forest resources (trees, wildlife, water) in a planned way to ensure they continue to serve human needs without being destroyed.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">1. Meaning of Forest Management</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">Forest management involves the planning, protection, harvesting and regeneration of forest resources so that the forest continues to provide timber, non-timber products, environmental services and habitat over the long term, rather than being exhausted.</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">2. Importance of Forests to Agriculture and the Economy</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">Forests moderate local climate and rainfall patterns, prevent soil erosion and desertification, serve as a source of timber, fuelwood, medicine and fodder, provide habitats that support biodiversity and natural pest control, generate revenue and employment (timber, tourism), and act as watersheds that protect rivers used for irrigation.</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">3. Methods of Forest Management</h3>\n<div class="learn-table-wrap">\n  <table class="learn-table">\n    <thead><tr><th>Method</th><th>Description</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Afforestation</td><td>Planting trees on land that previously had no forest</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Reafforestation</td><td>Replanting trees on land that was previously forested and cleared</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Controlled exploitation</td><td>Regulated, selective felling of mature trees, allowing regrowth</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Taungya system</td><td>Farmers grow food crops between young forest trees until canopy closes, tending trees in return for land use</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Forest reservation</td><td>Setting aside protected areas (forest reserves) where exploitation is restricted or banned</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">4. Benefits of Forest Management Practices</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">Effective forest management sustains timber supply for future generations, protects watersheds and soil from erosion, conserves biodiversity and wildlife habitats, and, through systems like taungya, allows farmers to earn food and income while young trees establish.</p>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Distinguish <em>afforestation</em> (planting trees where there was previously no forest) from <em>reafforestation</em> (replanting after forest has been cleared) — WAEC often tests this distinction.\n</div>\n',
