    contentHTML:
      '\n<div class="learn-intro">\n  Once established, ornamental plants require regular <span class="learn-keyword">maintenance practices</span> to keep them healthy, attractive and free from pests and diseases.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">1. Weeding</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">Removing unwanted plants competing with ornamentals for water, nutrients, light and space. Regular weeding also improves the appearance of the display and reduces pest/disease harbourage.</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">2. Watering</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">Ornamental plants need adequate, regular watering, especially during dry periods and shortly after transplanting, to support healthy root and shoot growth. Overwatering, however, can cause root rot.</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">3. Pruning</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">Cutting away dead, diseased or overcrowded branches/stems to maintain shape, encourage new growth and flowering, and improve air circulation within the plant.</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">4. Staking</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">Providing support (stakes, poles, trellises) to weak-stemmed or climbing plants to keep them upright, prevent lodging (falling over), and guide their growth direction.</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">5. Mulching</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">Covering the soil around plants with organic or synthetic material to conserve soil moisture, suppress weeds, regulate soil temperature, and, as organic mulch decomposes, add nutrients to the soil.</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">6. Manuring</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">Applying organic or inorganic fertilizers to replenish soil nutrients, ensuring vigorous growth, healthy foliage and abundant flowering.</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">7. Pest and Disease Control</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">Regular inspection for pests (aphids, caterpillars) and diseases (fungal spots, mildew) followed by appropriate cultural, biological or chemical control measures to keep plants healthy.</p>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Remember that mulching serves multiple purposes at once — moisture conservation, weed suppression, temperature regulation, and (for organic mulch) nutrient addition.\n</div>\n',
