    contentHTML:
      '\n<div class="learn-intro">\n  A <span class="learn-keyword">crop disease</span> is any abnormal condition that disrupts the normal structure or function of a plant, usually caused by pathogenic organisms or unfavourable conditions, leading to reduced yield and quality.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">1. Meaning of Crop Diseases</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">A crop disease is a harmful disturbance of a plant\'s normal growth and functioning, caused by living pathogens (fungi, bacteria, viruses, nematodes) or non-living factors (nutrient deficiency, extreme weather).</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">2. Types of Crop Diseases (by cause)</h3>\n<div class="learn-table-wrap">\n  <table class="learn-table">\n    <thead><tr><th>Type</th><th>Cause</th><th>Example</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Fungal</td><td>Fungi (moulds, rusts, smuts)</td><td>Cassava anthracnose, maize rust</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Bacterial</td><td>Bacteria</td><td>Bacterial wilt of tomato</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Viral</td><td>Viruses, often spread by insect vectors</td><td>Cassava mosaic virus</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Nematode</td><td>Microscopic roundworms</td><td>Root-knot nematode disease</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">3. Symptoms of Crop Diseases</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">Common symptoms include leaf spots, yellowing (chlorosis), wilting, stunted growth, rotting of roots/tubers/fruits, mosaic patterns on leaves, abnormal swellings (galls), and premature leaf/fruit drop.</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">4. Effects of Crop Diseases</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">Diseases reduce crop yield and quality, lower market value of produce, increase production costs (due to control measures), can spread rapidly to healthy plants, and in severe cases cause total crop failure, threatening food security and farmer income.</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">5. Control Measures</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">Control involves using disease-resistant/certified disease-free planting materials, crop rotation, proper field sanitation (removing infected plant debris), timely application of appropriate fungicides/bactericides, controlling insect vectors that spread viral diseases, and proper spacing for good air circulation.</p>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Remember that viral crop diseases are usually spread by insect vectors (e.g., whiteflies, aphids) — so controlling the vector is often the most effective control strategy, since there is no direct chemical cure for a plant virus.\n</div>\n',
