    term: 'Second Term',
    contentHTML:
      '\n<div class="learn-intro">\n  A <span class="learn-keyword">weed</span> is any unwanted plant growing where it is not desired, competing with crops for essential resources.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">1. Meaning of Weeds</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">A weed is simply a plant growing where it is not wanted — it may otherwise be a useful plant elsewhere, but in a crop field it interferes with cultivation and crop growth.</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">2. Types of Weeds</h3>\n<div class="learn-table-wrap">\n  <table class="learn-table">\n    <thead><tr><th>Type</th><th>Description</th><th>Example</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Annual weeds</td><td>Complete life cycle within one growing season</td><td>Goat weed (Ageratum)</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Biennial weeds</td><td>Complete life cycle in two years</td><td>Wild carrot</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Perennial weeds</td><td>Live for more than two years, often regrow from roots/rhizomes</td><td>Spear grass (Imperata), Nutgrass</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Broadleaf weeds</td><td>Have wide, net-veined leaves</td><td>Pigweed</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Grass weeds</td><td>Narrow, parallel-veined leaves like grasses</td><td>Elephant grass</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">3. Characteristics of Weeds</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">Weeds typically produce many seeds, germinate and grow rapidly, are highly adaptable to different environments, may reproduce vegetatively (through roots/rhizomes), and can persist in the soil as a seed bank for long periods.</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">4. Harmful and Beneficial Effects of Weeds</h3>\n<p class="learn-p"><strong>Harmful effects:</strong> competition with crops for water, nutrients, light and space; harbouring pests and disease pathogens; reducing crop yield and quality; increasing cost of production (labour for weeding). <strong>Beneficial effects:</strong> some weeds prevent soil erosion, add organic matter when ploughed under, serve as fodder for livestock, and a few have medicinal value.</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">5. Methods of Weed Control</h3>\n<p class="learn-p"><strong>Cultural:</strong> crop rotation, close spacing, mulching, timely planting. <strong>Mechanical:</strong> hand pulling, hoeing, slashing, ploughing/harrowing. <strong>Chemical:</strong> use of herbicides (selective or non-selective). <strong>Biological:</strong> use of natural enemies (insects, grazing animals) to suppress specific weed species.</p>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> WAEC likes to test the difference between a "selective" herbicide (kills specific weed types without harming the crop) and a "non-selective" herbicide (kills all vegetation it contacts).\n</div>\n',
