    contentHTML:
      '\n<div class="learn-intro">\n  Understanding the <span class="learn-keyword">anatomy</span> (structure) and <span class="learn-keyword">physiology</span> (function) of farm animals — especially cattle, poultry and pigs — helps farmers manage their health, nutrition and productivity effectively.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">1. External Parts of Farm Animals</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">Common external features studied include the head, horns (in cattle/goats), limbs and hooves, tail, udder (in females of milk-producing species), and in poultry, the comb, wattle, beak, and feathers.</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">2. The Digestive System</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">In ruminants (cattle), the stomach has four chambers — <strong>rumen</strong> (fermentation of fibrous feed by microbes), <strong>reticulum</strong> (traps foreign objects, forms cud), <strong>omasum</strong> (absorbs water), and <strong>abomasum</strong> (true stomach, acid digestion). In pigs and poultry (monogastrics), digestion occurs in a single-chambered stomach, with poultry additionally having a crop (storage) and gizzard (grinding) since they lack teeth.</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">3. The Circulatory System</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">The heart pumps blood through arteries, veins and capillaries, delivering oxygen and nutrients to body tissues and removing waste products. It consists of chambers (atria and ventricles) that work together to maintain continuous blood flow.</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">4. The Respiratory System</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">Air enters through the nostrils, passes through the trachea (windpipe) into the lungs, where oxygen is exchanged for carbon dioxide across tiny air sacs (alveoli in mammals; air sacs and lungs in birds, which have a highly efficient one-way airflow system).</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">5. The Reproductive System</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">Males possess testes (sperm production) and associated ducts/glands; females possess ovaries (egg production), oviducts/fallopian tubes, and a uterus (site of pregnancy in mammals) or, in poultry, a single functional ovary and oviduct that forms the egg.</p>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Learn the order of the ruminant stomach chambers: Rumen → Reticulum → Omasum → Abomasum (RROA) — WAEC frequently tests the sequence and function of each chamber.\n</div>\n',
