    term: 'Second Term',
    contentHTML:
      '\n<div class="learn-intro">\n  <span class="learn-keyword">Animal reproduction</span> is the biological process by which farm animals produce offspring, essential for maintaining and expanding livestock populations.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">1. Meaning and Importance of Animal Reproduction</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">Reproduction ensures the continuity of livestock species, allows farmers to increase herd/flock size, enables genetic improvement through selective breeding, and is the basis of products like milk (which follows calving) and meat animals (which must first be bred and born).</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">2. Types of Reproduction</h3>\n<p class="learn-p"><strong>Sexual reproduction</strong> (the norm in farm animals) involves the fusion of a sperm from a male and an egg (ovum) from a female to form a zygote. <strong>Asexual reproduction</strong> is rare in farm animals but includes processes like fission or budding in lower organisms; it is not typical of common livestock.</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">3. Reproductive Processes in Farm Animals</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">The key stages are: <strong>oestrus (heat)</strong> — the period when the female is receptive to mating; <strong>mating/service</strong> — union of male and female (natural or via artificial insemination); <strong>fertilisation</strong> — fusion of sperm and egg in the female reproductive tract; <strong>gestation</strong> — the period the developing offspring is carried in the uterus; and <strong>parturition</strong> — the act of giving birth.</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">4. Fertilisation and Gestation</h3>\n<div class="learn-table-wrap">\n  <table class="learn-table">\n    <thead><tr><th>Animal</th><th>Approx. Gestation Period</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Cattle</td><td>About 9 months (280 days)</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Goat</td><td>About 5 months (150 days)</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Sheep</td><td>About 5 months (147-150 days)</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Pig</td><td>About 3 months, 3 weeks, 3 days (114 days)</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Rabbit</td><td>About 1 month (30-31 days)</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n<p class="learn-p">In poultry, fertilisation occurs internally before the egg is laid, and development of the chick continues externally during incubation (about 21 days for chickens) rather than gestation in a uterus.</p>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Remember that poultry do not undergo gestation like mammals — fertilisation happens inside the hen, but embryo development mainly occurs outside the body during incubation of the laid egg.\n</div>\n',
