    contentHTML:
      '\n<div class="learn-intro">\n  <span class="learn-keyword">Environmental physiology</span> studies how climatic factors — temperature, rainfall, humidity, and wind — affect the growth, production and reproduction of farm animals, and how animals adapt to these conditions.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">1. Effects of Temperature</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">High temperatures can cause heat stress, reducing feed intake, growth rate, milk yield, egg production and fertility. Extremely low temperatures increase energy expenditure for maintaining body heat, which can also reduce productivity if not managed.</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">2. Effects of Rainfall and Humidity</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">Excessive rainfall and humidity can promote the growth of pathogens and parasites (e.g., ticks, worms), cause dampness in housing leading to respiratory diseases, and reduce feed/forage quality. Very low rainfall can limit pasture growth and water availability.</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">3. Effects of Wind</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">Strong winds increase heat loss from animals in cold conditions (wind chill) and can spread airborne diseases and parasites between farms; on the other hand, gentle air movement helps cool animals in hot weather through evaporative cooling.</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">4. Adaptation Strategies</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">Farmers help animals cope with climatic stress through proper housing design (shade, ventilation, insulation), providing adequate clean water especially in hot weather, choosing heat-tolerant or cold-tolerant breeds suited to the local climate, adjusting feeding times to cooler parts of the day, and controlling humidity/dampness in housing.</p>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Remember that heat stress mainly reduces feed intake and productivity (milk, eggs, growth, fertility) — WAEC often asks for the specific productivity traits affected by high temperature.\n</div>\n',
