    contentHTML:
      '\n<div class="learn-intro">\n  <span class="learn-keyword">Livestock management</span> refers to the set of practices used to care for farm animals — including housing, feeding, breeding and healthcare — to achieve efficient, profitable and sustainable production.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">1. Meaning and Principles of Livestock Management</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">Livestock management involves organising and applying scientific and practical knowledge to raise healthy, productive animals. Key principles include providing balanced nutrition, suitable housing, regular health care, appropriate breeding practices, and good record keeping.</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">2. Housing</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">Good housing protects animals from harsh weather and predators, provides adequate space to reduce stress and disease spread, ensures proper ventilation and drainage, and is designed according to the specific needs of the species (e.g., deep litter or battery cages for poultry, paddocks/pens for cattle).</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">3. Feeding</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">Animals must be provided with balanced rations appropriate to their species, age and production stage (growth, lactation, breeding). Clean water must always be available. Feed should be free from contamination and stored properly.</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">4. Breeding</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">Involves selecting suitable breeding stock, timing of mating/service, and choosing appropriate methods (natural mating or artificial insemination) to improve herd/flock quality and productivity over generations.</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">5. Healthcare</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">Includes routine vaccination, deworming, parasite control, prompt treatment of sick animals, quarantine of new or sick animals, and regular monitoring for early signs of disease.</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">6. Management Practices for Specific Livestock</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">Cattle, sheep and goats require adequate grazing/pasture or supplementary feed, secure housing/kraals, and regular deworming. Pigs need clean housing (piggeries), balanced concentrate feed, and good sanitation to prevent disease. Poultry require well-ventilated houses, balanced feed appropriate to their stage (chick mash, grower, layer mash), and strict biosecurity. Rabbits need clean, well-ventilated hutches and a diet of greens and concentrates.</p>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> Good livestock management combines proper housing, balanced feeding, sound breeding decisions and consistent healthcare — the foundation for healthy, productive, and profitable livestock enterprises.\n</div>\n',
