    contentHTML:
      '\n<div class="learn-intro">\n  <span class="learn-keyword">Rangeland</span> refers to extensive natural or semi-natural grasslands and shrublands used primarily for grazing livestock, and its careful management is essential to sustain both animal production and the land itself.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">1. Meaning of Rangeland</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">Rangeland is land covered by natural vegetation (grasses, shrubs) suitable for grazing or browsing by livestock and wildlife, typically not intensively cultivated or irrigated.</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">2. Types of Rangeland</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">Rangelands include natural grasslands/savannas, shrublands, and some forested areas used for grazing. In Nigeria, the Guinea and Sudan savanna zones provide extensive rangeland used for cattle, sheep and goat grazing.</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">3. Importance of Rangeland</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">Rangelands provide low-cost natural forage for grazing livestock, support pastoral/nomadic livestock production systems, help conserve wildlife and biodiversity, and, when well managed, protect soil from erosion.</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">4. Methods of Rangeland Improvement</h3>\n<div class="learn-table-wrap">\n  <table class="learn-table">\n    <thead><tr><th>Method</th><th>Description</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Reseeding</td><td>Planting improved/nutritious grass and legume species on degraded range</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Rotational grazing</td><td>Moving animals between paddocks/sections to allow vegetation to recover</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Controlled stocking</td><td>Limiting the number of animals grazed per hectare to match forage availability (avoiding overgrazing)</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Fertilizer application</td><td>Applying fertilizer to boost pasture growth and quality</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Bush/fire control</td><td>Controlled burning or clearing to manage unwanted woody plants and encourage grass regrowth</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">5. Effects of Overgrazing</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">Overgrazing (too many animals on too little land for too long) leads to loss of vegetation cover, soil erosion, reduced pasture productivity, and eventual desertification if not corrected.</p>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Rotational grazing and controlled stocking are the two most commonly tested rangeland improvement methods — both aim to prevent overgrazing by matching animal numbers/movement to what the land can sustainably support.\n</div>\n',
