    topic: 'Basic Economic Principles',
    topicCode: 'SS2-AGR-21',
    module: 'Agricultural Economics',
    term: 'Third Term',
    contentHTML:
      '\n<div class="learn-intro">\n  Agricultural economics applies core <span class="learn-keyword">economic principles</span> — scarcity, choice, opportunity cost, and the production possibilities curve — to farming decisions.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">1. Scarcity</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">Scarcity is the basic economic problem that resources (land, labour, capital) are limited relative to the unlimited wants of people. Because farm resources are scarce, farmers cannot produce unlimited quantities of every crop or livestock product.</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">2. Choice</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">Because of scarcity, farmers must make choices about what to produce, how much to produce, and how to allocate limited resources among competing uses (e.g., planting maize vs cassava on the same plot).</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">3. Opportunity Cost</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">Opportunity cost is the value of the next best alternative given up when a choice is made. For example, if a farmer uses land to grow maize instead of cassava, the opportunity cost is the cassava output that was forgone.</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">4. Scale of Preference</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">A scale of preference is a list of a person\'s wants arranged in order of importance, from the most pressing to the least. It helps farmers (and consumers) decide which needs to satisfy first when resources are limited.</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">5. Production Possibilities Curve (PPC)</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">The PPC is a graph showing the maximum possible combinations of two goods (e.g., maize and cassava) that can be produced with given, fixed resources and technology. Points on the curve represent full and efficient resource use; points inside the curve represent underutilised resources; points outside are currently unattainable.</p>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Always connect scarcity → choice → opportunity cost in that order: scarcity forces choice, and every choice has an opportunity cost (the next best alternative given up).\n</div>\n',
