    term: 'Third Term',
    contentHTML:
      '\n<div class="learn-intro">\n  <span class="learn-keyword">Supply</span> refers to the quantity of a good or service that producers are willing and able to offer for sale at a given price, within a specific period.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">1. Meaning of Supply</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">Supply reflects producers\' willingness and capacity to bring goods (e.g., farm produce) to the market at various prices. It is influenced by production costs, technology, and the profitability of production.</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">2. The Law of Supply</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">The law of supply states that, all other factors being constant, as the price of a good rises, the quantity supplied also rises, and as price falls, quantity supplied falls. This creates a direct (positive) relationship between price and quantity supplied.</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">3. Supply Schedule and Supply Curve</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">A <strong>supply schedule</strong> is a table showing the quantity of a good producers are willing to supply at various prices. A <strong>supply curve</strong> is its graphical representation, typically sloping upward from left to right, reflecting the positive price-quantity relationship.</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">4. Factors Affecting Supply</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">Besides price, supply is influenced by the cost of production inputs (seeds, fertilizer, labour), weather and climatic conditions, level of technology, government policies (subsidies, taxes), and the number of producers/farmers in the market.</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">5. Elasticity of Supply in Agriculture</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">Agricultural supply is often relatively inelastic in the short run because crops take time to grow (a farmer cannot instantly produce more maize in response to a price rise this week) but tends to be more elastic in the long run, as farmers can adjust land allocation and inputs over successive seasons.</p>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Unlike demand, the law of supply shows a DIRECT (positive) relationship between price and quantity supplied — this is why the supply curve slopes upward, unlike the downward-sloping demand curve.\n</div>\n',
